<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<chapter xml:id="u1-vectors-matrices-linear-maps" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Vectors, matrices, and linear maps</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      Vectors can represent displacements in space, but in this course they will more often represent data or model parameters. A word, document, image, or user can be encoded as a vector. Matrices then act on vectors, and the matrix-vector product gives the first large family of functions we study: linear maps.
    </p>
    <p>
      <em>Big questions.</em> How do vectors represent data, how do matrices act on vectors, and what is special about linear functions?
    </p>
    <p>
      <em>Learning outcomes.</em> By the end of this unit, students should be able to:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><em>U1-LO1.</em> Compute and interpret vector norms, dot products, distances, orthogonality, and cosine similarity.</li>
      <li><em>U1-LO2.</em> Represent objects or data as vectors and explain what cosine similarity and Euclidean distance measure differently.</li>
      <li><em>U1-LO3.</em> Perform basic matrix operations and check dimensions for matrix, vector, and matrix-product expressions.</li>
      <li><em>U1-LO4.</em> Compute <m>Ax</m> and interpret it both as row dot products and as a linear combination of the columns of <m>A</m>.</li>
      <li><em>U1-LO5.</em> Identify, construct, and interpret matrix representations of linear transformations.</li>
      <li><em>U1-LO6.</em> Compute small matrix products and explain how multiplication represents composition of linear maps.</li>
      <li><em>U1-LO7.</em> Distinguish linear maps from affine maps such as <m>\mathbf{x}\mapsto W\mathbf{x}+\mathbf{b}</m>.</li>
      <li><em>U1-LO8.</em> Interpret simple code, tables, or diagrams involving cosine similarity, attention-style weighted averages, and applying one matrix to many inputs.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      <em>Toolbox skills.</em> Compute vector norms, distances, dot products, cosine similarities, matrix sums, scalar multiples, transposes, matrix-vector products, small matrix products, and simple weighted averages.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <xi:include href="sec-u1-vector-geometry.ptx" />
  <xi:include href="sec-u1-matrix-operations.ptx" />
  <xi:include href="sec-u1-matrix-vector-geometric-actions.ptx" />
  <xi:include href="sec-u1-matrix-multiplication-composition.ptx" />

  <section xml:id="sec-u1-apps-computation">
    <title>Coding recap</title>
    <subsection xml:id="lab-u1-callout">
      <title>Linked notebook</title>
      <p>
        Run <url href="https://sebroc.github.io/MATH345-Course-Materials/labs/#lab-u1">Lab U1: Vectors, similarity, attention, and matrix actions</url>. The main sequence practices vector applications, document similarity, token attention, matrix-vector products, geometric matrix actions, applying one matrix to many points, composition, affine maps, and short code outputs. The final part contains review questions and a short forward look to square-grid visualizations.
      </p>
    <p>
      For a quick reference on arrays, matrix products, norms, rankings, and slicing, see the programming appendix sections <xref ref="appcomp-arrays-shapes" text="global"/>, <xref ref="appcomp-elementwise-vs-linear-algebra" text="global"/>, <xref ref="appcomp-dot-norm-cosim" text="global"/>, <xref ref="appcomp-sorting-rankings" text="global"/>, and <xref ref="appcomp-indexing-slicing" text="global"/>.
    </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="sec-u1-review-activities">
      <title>Review activities</title>
      <activity xml:id="act-u1-recap-ranking">
        <title>Reading a ranking</title>
        <statement>
          <p>Continuing the cosine-similarity ranking from <xref ref="code-u1-word-count-vectors"/>, the code is</p>
          <program language="python"><code><![CDATA[scores = np.array([1.0, 0.5, 0.0])
doc_names = np.array(["D1", "D2", "D3"])
ranking = doc_names[np.argsort(scores)[::-1]]
ranking
]]></code></program>
          <ol>
            <li><p>What is the value of <c>ranking</c>?</p></li>
            <li><p>What is being ranked?</p></li>
            <li><p>Is this ranking based on distance or direction?</p></li>
          </ol>
        </statement>
        <solution>
          <p>
            The value is <c>["D1", "D2", "D3"]</c>. The documents are being ranked by cosine similarity with the query. This is a direction-based ranking because cosine similarity compares direction after normalization.
          </p>
        </solution>
      </activity>
      <activity xml:id="act-u1-recap-attention">
        <title>Reading an attention computation</title>
        <statement>
          <p>In <xref ref="code-u1-token-attention"/>, the code was</p>
          <program language="python"><code><![CDATA[s = K @ q
alpha = s / s.sum()
out = alpha @ V
]]></code></program>
          <ol>
            <li><p>What does <c>s</c> contain?</p></li>
            <li><p>Why do the entries of <c>alpha</c> add to <m>1</m>?</p></li>
            <li><p>What does <c>out</c> represent?</p></li>
          </ol>
        </statement>
        <solution>
          <p>
            The vector <c>s</c> contains dot-product scores. The vector <c>alpha</c> contains weights because the scores were divided by their sum. The vector <c>out</c> is a weighted average of the value vectors stored in the rows of <c>V</c>.
          </p>
        </solution>
      </activity>
      <activity xml:id="act-u1-recap-geometry">
        <title>Reading a geometric computation</title>
        <statement>
          <p>In <xref ref="code-u1-matrix-many-points"/>, the code was</p>
          <program language="python"><code><![CDATA[X = np.array([
    [0, 1, 1, 0, 0],
    [0, 0, 1, 1, 0],
], dtype=float)

A = np.array([
    [1, 1],
    [0, 1],
], dtype=float)

Y = A @ X
Y
]]></code></program>
          <ol>
            <li><p>What are the columns of <c>X</c>?</p></li>
            <li><p>What are the columns of <c>Y</c>?</p></li>
            <li><p>Which transformation from the gallery is this?</p></li>
          </ol>
        </statement>
        <solution>
          <p>
            The columns of <c>X</c> are vertices of the unit square. The columns of <c>Y</c> are their images under <m>A</m>. This is a horizontal shear because <m>A</m> sends <m>(x,y)</m> to <m>(x+y,y)</m>.
          </p>
        </solution>
      </activity>
      <activity xml:id="act-u1-recap-composition">
        <title>Reading composition order</title>
        <statement>
          <program language="python"><code><![CDATA[S = np.array([
    [2., 0.],
    [0., 1.],
])

R = np.array([
    [0., -1.],
    [1.,  0.],
])

R @ S, S @ R
]]></code></program>
          <ol>
            <li><p>Which product applies <c>S</c> first and then <c>R</c>?</p></li>
            <li><p>Are the two products equal?</p></li>
            <li><p>What does the difference mean geometrically?</p></li>
          </ol>
        </statement>
        <solution>
          <p>
            The product <c>R @ S</c> applies <c>S</c> first and then <c>R</c>, because the rightmost matrix acts first. The two products are not equal:
            <me>
              RS=\begin{bmatrix}0\amp -1\\2\amp0\end{bmatrix},
              \qquad
              SR=\begin{bmatrix}0\amp -2\\1\amp0\end{bmatrix}.
            </me>
            Rotating after a horizontal stretch is not the same as stretching after a rotation.
          </p>
        </solution>
      </activity>
      <activity xml:id="act-u1-recap-affine">
        <title>Reading an affine rule</title>
        <statement>
          <program language="python"><code><![CDATA[W = np.array([
    [1., 1.],
    [0., 2.],
])

b = np.array([3., -1.])

def f(x):
    return W @ x + b
]]></code></program>
          <p>Is <c>f</c> linear? Justify your answer using the zero vector.</p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
          <p>
            No. A linear map must send <m>\mathbf{0}</m> to <m>\mathbf{0}</m>. But <m>f(\mathbf{0})=W\mathbf{0}+\mathbf{b}=\mathbf{b}</m>, and <m>\mathbf{b}</m> is not the zero vector. The rule is affine, but not linear.
          </p>
        </solution>
      </activity>
    </subsection>
  </section>

