    <subsection xml:id="u1-exercises-applied-geometric-computational">
      <title>Additional exercises</title>
      <p>
        These exercises connect the main Unit 1 ideas: vectors as data, cosine similarity, weighted averages, matrix-vector products, geometric matrix actions, composition, affine maps, and code interpretation. Solutions are collected in Appendix <xref ref="app-u1-applied-solutions" text="global"/>.
      </p>
      <exercise xml:id="ex-u1-applied-doc-vectors-cosine-distance">
        <title>Word-count vectors and cosine similarity</title>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Use the dictionary <c>linear</c>, <c>matrix</c>, <c>data</c>. Let
            <me>\mathbf{q}=\begin{bmatrix}1\\0\\1\end{bmatrix},</me>
            <me>\mathbf{D}_1=\begin{bmatrix}2\\0\\2\end{bmatrix},\qquad \mathbf{D}_2=\begin{bmatrix}1\\1\\0\end{bmatrix},\qquad \mathbf{D}_3=\begin{bmatrix}0\\2\\0\end{bmatrix}.</me>
          </p>
          <ol marker="a">
            <li><p>Compute the cosine similarity between <m>\mathbf{q}</m> and each document vector.</p></li>
            <li><p>Rank the documents by cosine similarity.</p></li>
            <li><p>Explain why <m>\mathbf{D}_1</m> has cosine similarity <m>1</m> with <m>\mathbf{q}</m>.</p></li>
            <li><p>Which document is orthogonal to <m>\mathbf{q}</m>?</p></li>
            <li><p>What does that orthogonality mean in terms of the dictionary words?</p></li>
            <li><p>Compute the Euclidean distance from <m>\mathbf{q}</m> to each <m>\mathbf{D}_i</m>. Does the distance ranking match the cosine ranking?</p></li>
          </ol>
          <p><em>Learning outcomes.</em> U1-LO1, U1-LO2.</p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>
      <exercise xml:id="ex-u1-applied-weighted-average-values">
        <title>Weighted average of value vectors</title>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Let
            <me>\mathbf{v}_1=\begin{bmatrix}10\\0\end{bmatrix},\qquad \mathbf{v}_2=\begin{bmatrix}0\\10\end{bmatrix},\qquad \mathbf{v}_3=\begin{bmatrix}10\\10\end{bmatrix},</me>
            and let
            <me>\boldsymbol{\alpha}=\begin{bmatrix}\frac14\\\frac14\\\frac12\end{bmatrix}.</me>
          </p>
          <ol marker="a">
            <li><p>Compute <m>\alpha_1\mathbf{v}_1+\alpha_2\mathbf{v}_2+\alpha_3\mathbf{v}_3</m>.</p></li>
            <li><p>Explain why this is a weighted average.</p></li>
            <li><p>Which value vector receives the largest weight?</p></li>
          </ol>
          <p><em>Learning outcomes.</em> U1-LO8.</p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>
      <exercise xml:id="ex-u1-applied-attention-kq-scores">
        <title>Attention bridge: hand scores versus <m>K\mathbf{q}</m></title>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Use the token attention activity with tokens <q>small</q>, <q>red</q>, <q>bird</q>. Let
            <me>\mathbf{q}=\begin{bmatrix}1\\1\end{bmatrix},</me>
            <me>\mathbf{k}_{\mathrm{small}}=\begin{bmatrix}1\\0\end{bmatrix},\qquad \mathbf{k}_{\mathrm{red}}=\begin{bmatrix}0\\1\end{bmatrix},\qquad \mathbf{k}_{\mathrm{bird}}=\begin{bmatrix}1\\1\end{bmatrix}.</me>
          </p>
          <ol marker="a">
            <li>
              <p>
                Compute the three dot products
                <me>\mathbf{q}\cdot \mathbf{k}_{\mathrm{small}},\qquad \mathbf{q}\cdot \mathbf{k}_{\mathrm{red}},\qquad \mathbf{q}\cdot \mathbf{k}_{\mathrm{bird}}.</me>
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                Put the key vectors as rows of the matrix
                <me>K=\begin{bmatrix}1\amp 0\\ 0\amp 1\\ 1\amp 1\end{bmatrix}.</me>
                Compute <m>K\mathbf{q}</m>.
              </p>
            </li>
            <li><p>Explain why the entries of <m>K\mathbf{q}</m> are the same scores from part (a).</p></li>
            <li><p>Normalize the scores by dividing by their sum. What is the weight vector <m>\boldsymbol{\alpha}</m>?</p></li>
          </ol>
          <p><em>Learning outcomes.</em> U1-LO1, U1-LO4, U1-LO8.</p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>
      <exercise xml:id="ex-u1-applied-rows-measure-columns-contribute">
        <title>Rows measure; columns contribute</title>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Let
            <me>
              A=\begin{bmatrix}-1\amp 4\amp -5\\ 3\amp 1\amp -2\end{bmatrix},
              \qquad
              \mathbf{x}=\begin{bmatrix}2\\ -3\\ 4\end{bmatrix}.
            </me>
          </p>
          <ol marker="a">
            <li><p>Compute <m>A\mathbf{x}</m> using row dot products.</p></li>
            <li><p>Compute <m>A\mathbf{x}</m> as a linear combination of the columns of <m>A</m>.</p></li>
            <li><p>In this example, what does the first row of <m>A</m> measure?</p></li>
            <li><p>In this example, what do the coordinates <m>2,-3,4</m> tell you in the column-combination view?</p></li>
          </ol>
          <p><em>Learning outcomes.</em> U1-LO4.</p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>
      <exercise xml:id="ex-u1-applied-unit-square-test">
        <title>The unit-square visualization</title>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Let
            <me>A=\begin{bmatrix}2\amp -1\\ 1\amp 1\end{bmatrix}.</me>
          </p>
          <ol marker="a">
            <li><p>Compute <m>A\mathbf{e}_1</m> and <m>A\mathbf{e}_2</m>.</p></li>
            <li><p>Compute <m>A\begin{bmatrix}1\\1\end{bmatrix}</m>.</p></li>
            <li>
              <p>
                Apply <m>A</m> to the four corners of the unit square:
                <me>\begin{bmatrix}0\\0\end{bmatrix},\qquad \begin{bmatrix}1\\0\end{bmatrix},\qquad \begin{bmatrix}1\\1\end{bmatrix},\qquad \begin{bmatrix}0\\1\end{bmatrix}.</me>
              </p>
            </li>
            <li><p>Sketch the image of the unit square.</p></li>
            <li><p>Which two transformed edges leaving the origin are the columns of <m>A</m>?</p></li>
          </ol>
          <p><em>Learning outcomes.</em> U1-LO5, U1-LO8.</p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>
      <exercise xml:id="ex-u1-applied-transformation-matching">
        <title>Transformation matching</title>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Match each matrix with the correct geometric description.
            <me>
              A_1=\begin{bmatrix}1\amp 1\\ 0\amp 1\end{bmatrix},\qquad
              A_2=\begin{bmatrix}0\amp -1\\ 1\amp 0\end{bmatrix},\qquad
              A_3=\begin{bmatrix}1\amp 0\\ 0\amp 0\end{bmatrix},
            </me>
            <me>
              A_4=\begin{bmatrix}-1\amp 0\\ 0\amp 1\end{bmatrix},\qquad
              A_5=\begin{bmatrix}2\amp 0\\ 0\amp 1\end{bmatrix},\qquad
              A_6=\begin{bmatrix}0\amp 1\\ 1\amp 0\end{bmatrix}.
            </me>
          </p>
          <ol>
            <li><p>Reflection across the <m>y</m>-axis.</p></li>
            <li><p>Projection onto the <m>x</m>-axis.</p></li>
            <li><p>Horizontal stretch.</p></li>
            <li><p>Horizontal shear.</p></li>
            <li><p><m>90^\circ</m> counterclockwise rotation.</p></li>
            <li><p>Reflection across the line <m>y=x</m>.</p></li>
          </ol>
          <p>
            For each match, briefly justify your answer by describing what happens to <m>\mathbf{e}_1</m> and <m>\mathbf{e}_2</m>.
          </p>
          <p><em>Learning outcomes.</em> U1-LO5.</p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>
      <exercise xml:id="ex-u1-applied-composition-order">
        <title>Composition order: <m>RS</m> versus <m>SR</m></title>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Let
            <me>
              S=\begin{bmatrix}2\amp 0\\ 0\amp 1\end{bmatrix},
              \qquad
              R=\begin{bmatrix}0\amp -1\\ 1\amp 0\end{bmatrix}.
            </me>
          </p>
          <ol marker="a">
            <li><p>Compute <m>RS</m>.</p></li>
            <li><p>Compute <m>SR</m>.</p></li>
            <li><p>Are <m>RS</m> and <m>SR</m> equal?</p></li>
            <li><p>Which product represents <q>apply <m>S</m> first, then apply <m>R</m></q>?</p></li>
            <li><p>Explain geometrically why the two orders produce different results.</p></li>
          </ol>
          <p><em>Learning outcomes.</em> U1-LO6.</p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>
      <exercise xml:id="ex-u1-applied-affine-not-linear">
        <title>Affine but not linear</title>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Consider the transformation
            <me>T\begin{bmatrix}x\\y\end{bmatrix}=\begin{bmatrix}x+1\\y\end{bmatrix}.</me>
          </p>
          <ol marker="a">
            <li><p>Compute <m>T(0,0)</m>.</p></li>
            <li><p>Explain why <m>T</m> cannot be written in the form <m>T(\mathbf{x})=A\mathbf{x}</m> for any matrix <m>A</m>.</p></li>
            <li>
              <p>
                Write <m>T</m> in the affine form
                <me>T(\mathbf{x})=A\mathbf{x}+\mathbf{b}.</me>
              </p>
            </li>
            <li><p>Is <m>T</m> linear, affine but not linear, or neither?</p></li>
          </ol>
          <p><em>Learning outcomes.</em> U1-LO7.</p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>
      <exercise xml:id="ex-u1-applied-code-sorting-similarity">
        <title>Code interpretation: sorting similarity scores</title>
        <statement>
          <p>Consider the code:</p>
          <program language="python"><code><![CDATA[import numpy as np

doc_names = np.array(["D1", "D2", "D3"])
scores = np.array([1.0, 0.5, 0.0])
ranking = doc_names[np.argsort(scores)[::-1]]
ranking
]]></code></program>
          <ol marker="a">
            <li><p>What is the value of <c>ranking</c>?</p></li>
            <li><p>What does <c>np.argsort(scores)</c> return?</p></li>
            <li><p>What does <c>[::-1]</c> do?</p></li>
            <li><p>What is being ranked?</p></li>
            <li><p>Is this ranking based on direction, distance, or alphabetical order?</p></li>
          </ol>
          <p><em>Learning outcomes.</em> U1-LO2, U1-LO8.</p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>
      <exercise xml:id="ex-u1-applied-transformer-shape-check">
        <title>Transformer shape check</title>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Suppose a sequence of token vectors is stored in a matrix
            <me>X\in \mathbb{R}^{6\times 5}.</me>
            Let
            <me>W_Q,W_K,W_V\in \mathbb{R}^{5\times 2},</me>
            and define
            <me>Q=XW_Q,\qquad K=XW_K,\qquad V=XW_V,\qquad S=QK^T.</me>
            A later weighting step produces an attention-weight matrix <m>A_{\mathrm{att}}</m>, and the attention output is
            <me>H=A_{\mathrm{att}}V.</me>
          </p>
          <ol marker="a">
            <li><p>What are the shapes of <m>Q</m>, <m>K</m>, and <m>V</m>?</p></li>
            <li><p>What is the shape of <m>K^T</m>?</p></li>
            <li><p>What is the shape of <m>S=QK^T</m>?</p></li>
            <li><p>What is the shape of <m>A_{\mathrm{att}}</m>?</p></li>
            <li><p>What is the shape of <m>H</m>?</p></li>
            <li><p>What does the <m>(i,j)</m>-entry of <m>S</m> compute?</p></li>
            <li><p>Which product forms the weighted averages of value vectors?</p></li>
            <li><p>In this convention, are token vectors stored as rows or columns of <m>X</m>?</p></li>
          </ol>
          <p><em>Learning outcomes.</em> U1-LO3, U1-LO6, U1-LO8.</p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>
    </subsection>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="u1-highlights">
    <title>Unit 1 highlights</title>
    <subsection xml:id="u1-highlights-reference">
      <title>Mathematical quick reference</title>
      <paragraphs>
        <title>Vectors and linear combinations</title>
        <p><alert>Vector.</alert> An ordered list of scalars, <m>\mathbf v=(v_1,\ldots,v_n)\in\mathbb R^n</m>. Vectors are equal when their dimensions and corresponding entries agree. The zero vector has every entry zero.</p>
        <p><alert>Operations.</alert> For <m>\mathbf u,\mathbf v\in\mathbb R^n</m> and <m>c\in\mathbb R</m>,
          <me>\mathbf u+\mathbf v=(u_1+v_1,\ldots,u_n+v_n),\qquad c\mathbf v=(cv_1,\ldots,cv_n).</me>
        </p>
        <p><alert>Displacement.</alert> From a point <m>P</m> to a point <m>Q</m>, the displacement vector is <m>\overrightarrow{PQ}=Q-P</m>.</p>
        <p><alert>Linear combination.</alert> <m>c_1\mathbf v_1+\cdots+c_k\mathbf v_k</m>. It is a <alert>convex combination</alert> when every <m>c_i\geq0</m> and <m>\sum_i c_i=1</m>.</p>
      </paragraphs>
      <paragraphs>
        <title>Norm, dot product, and angle</title>
        <p><alert>Norm and dot product.</alert> For vectors of the same dimension,
          <me>\mathbf u\cdot\mathbf v=\sum_{i=1}^n u_iv_i,\qquad \|\mathbf v\|=\sqrt{\mathbf v\cdot\mathbf v},\qquad \operatorname{dist}(\mathbf u,\mathbf v)=\|\mathbf u-\mathbf v\|.</me>
        </p>
        <p><alert>Unit vector.</alert> A vector of norm <m>1</m>. Normalize a nonzero vector by <m>\mathbf v/\|\mathbf v\|</m>.</p>
        <p><alert>Angle and cosine similarity.</alert> For nonzero <m>\mathbf u,\mathbf v</m> and their angle <m>\theta\in[0,\pi]</m>,
          <me>\operatorname{cosim}(\mathbf u,\mathbf v)=\frac{\mathbf u\cdot\mathbf v}{\|\mathbf u\|\|\mathbf v\|}=\cos\theta\in[-1,1].</me>
          Angle and cosine similarity are undefined if either vector is zero.</p>
        <p><alert>Orthogonality.</alert> <m>\mathbf u\perp\mathbf v</m> means <m>\mathbf u\cdot\mathbf v=0</m>; for nonzero vectors, this is equivalent to a right angle.</p>
      </paragraphs>
      <paragraphs>
        <title>Matrix operations and dimensions</title>
        <p><alert>Matrix.</alert> <m>A=[a_{ij}]\in\mathbb R^{m\times n}</m> has <m>m</m> rows and <m>n</m> columns. Addition requires equal sizes and is entrywise, as is scalar multiplication. A square matrix has <m>m=n</m>.</p>
        <p><alert>Transpose.</alert> <m>A^T\in\mathbb R^{n\times m}</m> has entries <m>(A^T)_{ij}=a_{ji}</m>. A square matrix is symmetric when <m>A^T=A</m>.</p>
        <p><alert>Product.</alert> If <m>A\in\mathbb R^{m\times n}</m> and <m>B\in\mathbb R^{n\times p}</m>, then
          <me>AB\in\mathbb R^{m\times p},\qquad (AB)_{ij}=\sum_{k=1}^n a_{ik}b_{kj}.</me>
        </p>
        <p><alert>Identities.</alert> Whenever dimensions permit,
          <me>A(BC)=(AB)C,\qquad A(B+C)=AB+AC,\qquad (A+B)C=AC+BC,</me>
          <me>(AB)^T=B^TA^T,\qquad (A^T)^T=A,\qquad AI_n=I_mA=A.</me>
          In general <m>AB\neq BA</m>; matrices <alert>commute</alert> when both products exist and <m>AB=BA</m>.</p>
      </paragraphs>
      <paragraphs>
        <title>Matrix-vector products and linear maps</title>
        <p><alert>Two descriptions of a product.</alert> If <m>A=[\mathbf a_1\ \cdots\ \mathbf a_n]</m> and <m>\mathbf x\in\mathbb R^n</m>, then
          <me>A\mathbf x=x_1\mathbf a_1+\cdots+x_n\mathbf a_n,\qquad (A\mathbf x)_i=\sum_{j=1}^n a_{ij}x_j.</me>
          Columns contribute scaled vectors; each output entry is a row dot product.</p>
        <p><alert>Linear map.</alert> <m>T:\mathbb R^n\to\mathbb R^m</m> is linear when
          <me>T(\mathbf u+\mathbf v)=T(\mathbf u)+T(\mathbf v),\qquad T(c\mathbf v)=cT(\mathbf v).</me>
          In particular, <m>T(\mathbf0)=\mathbf0</m>. Its matrix is
          <me>A=[T(\mathbf e_1)\ \cdots\ T(\mathbf e_n)],\qquad T(\mathbf x)=A\mathbf x.</me>
        </p>
        <p><alert>Affine map.</alert> <m>F(\mathbf x)=A\mathbf x+\mathbf b</m> is linear exactly when <m>\mathbf b=\mathbf0</m>.</p>
        <p><alert>Composition.</alert> Applying <m>B</m> first and <m>A</m> second gives <m>\mathbf x\mapsto AB\mathbf x</m>. Check the inner dimensions before multiplying.</p>
      </paragraphs>
      <paragraphs>
        <title>Geometric matrix actions</title>
        <p><alert>Read the columns.</alert> In the plane, the columns of <m>A</m> are the images of <m>\mathbf e_1</m> and <m>\mathbf e_2</m>; they determine the image of the unit square.</p>
        <p><alert>Scaling and counterclockwise rotation.</alert>
          <me>D=\begin{bmatrix}a\amp0\\0\amp b\end{bmatrix},\qquad R_\theta=\begin{bmatrix}\cos\theta\amp-\sin\theta\\\sin\theta\amp\cos\theta\end{bmatrix}.</me>
        </p>
        <p><alert>Reflections and horizontal shear.</alert> Reflection across the <m>y</m>-axis, reflection across <m>y=x</m>, and the shear <m>(x,y)\mapsto(x+ky,y)</m> have matrices
          <me>\begin{bmatrix}-1\amp0\\0\amp1\end{bmatrix},\qquad \begin{bmatrix}0\amp1\\1\amp0\end{bmatrix},\qquad \begin{bmatrix}1\amp k\\0\amp1\end{bmatrix}.</me>
        </p>
      </paragraphs>
    </subsection>
    <subsection xml:id="u1-highlights-mistakes">
      <title>Common mistakes</title>
      <p>Confusing a dot product with a distance; using cosine similarity with a zero vector; omitting normalization; mixing up rows and columns; ignoring product dimensions; reversing composition order; assuming <m>AB=BA</m>; assuming that non-diagonal matrices cannot commute; or calling an affine map linear when its constant term is nonzero.</p>
    </subsection>
    <subsection xml:id="u1-highlights-connections">
      <title>Connections</title>
      <p><xref ref="u2-anatomy-linear-map" text="custom">Unit 2</xref> uses matrix equations to describe solution sets, column spaces, and null spaces. <xref ref="u3-beyond-linear-maps" text="custom">Unit 3</xref> uses affine maps for local approximation and dot products for directional derivatives. <xref ref="u4-orthogonality-least-squares" text="custom">Unit 4</xref> develops dot products into orthogonality and projection. <xref ref="u5-optimization-training" text="custom">Unit 5</xref> studies eigenvectors and quadratic forms of matrix maps.</p>
    </subsection>
  </section>
</chapter>
